WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:07.640
Subquery is a query inside another query.

00:07.640 --> 00:10.880
So what this means let's have a sketch to understand it.

00:10.880 --> 00:16.200
So so far what we have learned we have different database tables like the orders customers and so on.

00:16.200 --> 00:19.760
And we write a simple SQL queries like select from where.

00:19.800 --> 00:22.800
So the SQL can retrieve data from the database tables.

00:22.800 --> 00:25.640
And in the output we will get some kind of results.

00:25.640 --> 00:27.000
So this is so far what you have done.

00:27.000 --> 00:28.960
We have done very simple queries.

00:29.000 --> 00:32.000
Now in our query we can have things a little bit different.

00:32.000 --> 00:38.080
So we could have another query that is inside our query where we do the same things like select from

00:38.080 --> 00:38.520
where.

00:38.560 --> 00:43.080
So we have now a query inside our query and we call this embedded query.

00:43.080 --> 00:44.680
We call it a subquery.

00:44.680 --> 00:49.880
And the original query the first one where we have select from we call it main query.

00:49.880 --> 00:53.400
So now if you execute the whole query what's going to happen is SQL first is going to go and select

00:53.400 --> 00:54.320
the subquery.

00:54.320 --> 00:55.600
And then it's going to execute it.

00:55.600 --> 00:59.560
So it's going to go and select and retrieve data from our database tables.

00:59.560 --> 01:03.560
And the result of the subquery will not be sent to the users to us.

01:03.560 --> 01:04.840
So we cannot see it.

01:04.880 --> 01:05.680
What can happen.

01:05.680 --> 01:10.000
The result can stay inside the query as an intermediate results.

01:10.200 --> 01:16.760
And then now our main query can go and start interacting with this intermediate result from the subquery.

01:16.760 --> 01:21.760
So the main query going to do some kind of operations on top of this intermediate results and use it

01:21.760 --> 01:24.190
for filtering or joining or any purpose.

01:24.190 --> 01:28.550
And still the main query can go and query the original database tables.

01:28.550 --> 01:33.150
So now the main query has two sources for data the original database tables.

01:33.270 --> 01:36.310
And as well the result from another query.

01:36.310 --> 01:41.710
So now by looking to this you can see the subquery is a query inside the main query.

01:41.710 --> 01:44.110
And it's play a role of supporter.

01:44.150 --> 01:47.310
So it supports the main query with data.

01:47.310 --> 01:53.030
And the main job of the main query is of course to get all those data and to show us at the end the

01:53.030 --> 01:53.990
final results.

01:54.030 --> 01:59.550
Now there is now two things about this intermediate results that we got as a result from the subquery.

01:59.590 --> 02:05.830
Once the execution of the query is completely done, what can happen is going to go and destroy this

02:05.870 --> 02:06.750
intermediate result.

02:06.750 --> 02:08.310
So it's going to totally drop it.

02:08.310 --> 02:10.390
So we will not find it anywhere.

02:10.430 --> 02:11.550
It's completely lost.

02:11.590 --> 02:16.790
Now the other thing about the intermediate results is that imagine you are making another query that

02:16.790 --> 02:19.230
is completely outside of the first query.

02:19.270 --> 02:21.790
We are selecting few tables from our database.

02:21.830 --> 02:23.550
Now you might say, you know what?

02:23.590 --> 02:28.390
Is it possible to access the intermediate results from the first query.

02:28.390 --> 02:31.550
So now we are talking about completely external query.

02:31.590 --> 02:32.630
You cannot do that.

02:32.670 --> 02:38.510
The intermediate result of the subquery is only locally known from the main query itself.

02:38.550 --> 02:41.310
And it is not globally available for any other query.

02:41.310 --> 02:44.870
So the subquery can be used only from the main query.
